\title{OPT-E: where RLVM does more work than gruszka, unit by unit}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{arena}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Research package "OPT-E" of the second optimisation wave, 2026-11-25 (10:49 to 12:10 UTC). The question was left open by [the profile of the final default configuration](rep-P11), sections 5.2 to 5.4 and 8, and by [the integration of the first wave](rep-INT8): RLVM's machine needs 8 to 22 \% more ticks than gruszka on Mathlib, navier-stokes-euler and cslib, the time is flat over the units, and three observations had no explanation. This report joins the two checkers declaration by declaration with counters added to private copies of both, takes both apart by switching their mechanisms off one at a time, and reads the two implementations where the counts differ. Nothing in the repository was changed, no patch is delivered, no Oracle GraalVM was used. The evidence is counts; the only times are two settings of the shipped executable against itself in quiet windows (section 6) and times quoted from rep-P11.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The two tick counters do not count the same events, so "22 \% more ticks" is not 22 \% more work.} gruszka ticks at nine sites, and 42 to 51 \% of its ticks are entries of inference \em{before} the cache is asked: 25 to 37 \% of all its ticks are inference-cache hits. The machine never ticks on a memo hit, and ticks where gruszka does not: once per node of an inert application nest (19 to 23 \% of its ticks), per code block, per \code{DOM}, \code{INST}, \code{APPLY}, \code{CARGS}. The events that can be matched say: unfoldings through a body 1.32 times gruszka's on Mathlib, 1.86 on navier-stokes-euler, 1.45 on cslib (rep-P11 read 0.93 to 1.09 from the same counters by subtracting memo hits that are not part of the call count); whnf 1.8 to 2.3 times; comparisons past the tables 1.00 to 1.47 times; hash-consed records 1.8 to 2.2 times gruszka's values (section 2).}
\li{\strong{Checked in isolation, the two checkers unfold and compare alike.} With a session of one declaration and every fact that outlives a declaration switched off on both sides, navier-stokes-euler takes the machine 14.25 M unfoldings, 18.2 M comparisons and 7.6 M whnf, and gruszka 14.17 M unfoldings through a body, 17.1 M comparisons and 6.4 M whnf; cslib 12.07 M, 14.6 M and 8.2 M against 12.61 M, 16.4 M and 5.6 M. A small declaration checked alone by gruszka costs it what it costs the machine (1,432 ticks and 125 unfoldings against 1,410 and 123). Unfoldings agree within 4 \% and comparisons within 11 \%; only whnf stays 1.2 and 1.5 times gruszka's. The difference in reduction work is what is \em{remembered between declarations} (section 4).}
\li{\strong{What gruszka remembers and RLVM does not.} (a) Its session holds 8 MiB against 1 MiB. (b) A table of comparison results for pairs of \em{closed} values, both outcomes, kept across declarations and sessions, written when a comparison needed at least one unfolding: 144,000 to 305,000 entries per corpus, 209,000 to 656,000 hits. Without it gruszka's unfoldings rise by 23 \% on Mathlib, 35 \% on cslib and 26 \% on navier-stokes-euler; at the machine's session size of 1 MiB the table is worth 12 \% of gruszka's ticks on navier-stokes-euler and 10 \% on cslib and 39 and 34 \% of its unfoldings, and without it gruszka unfolds as often as the machine does (through a body: 6.45 M against 6.22 M and 9.68 M against 9.29 M). (c) Check facts for closed shared terms with level parameters, where RLVM's \code{pchk} has them only without: worth 2 to 6 \% of gruszka's ticks, and a shadow counter in the machine shows that the extension would answer 0.02 to 0.22 \% of the machine's ticks: not a lever (section 4).}
\li{\strong{Observation 1, the four eager-reduction proofs of \code{Batteries.Data.Char.Basic}}: no rule of gruszka decides them and the official kernel has no rule the machine lacks. The 1.0 to 3.2 M ticks are the machine's own \em{probes} of the switch \code{eager}, which evaluate what the official plain procedure never evaluates. RLVM's reference engine decides the four in 1,904 to 8,138 steps, gruszka in 1,467 to 2,550 ticks; gruszka has no eager-reduction mode at all. With a probe budget of 2^16 instead of 2^22 ticks the four are cut, declined and decided by the reference engine: 8.1 M ticks less on cslib and on Mathlib, 4.1 M less on con-leche (section 5.1).}
\li{\strong{Observation 2, the four \code{quadraticBilin} proofs}: gruszka hash-conses everything, checks every node once per environment it reads, and re-checks no shared subproof, exactly as the machine does (378,000 inference misses against 418,000 in the largest). What it does not do there is unfold: 8,600 unfoldings against the machine's 164,700. The machine's same-head probe is cut off by the cap of 2^18 ticks per declaration that the switch \code{congruence} carries, after which the official order unfolds both sides. Without the cap the four take 5.20 M ticks instead of 8.12 M and 9,300 unfoldings in the largest. gruszka's tick gets cheaper with the size of a unit because 63 \% of its ticks there are inference-cache hits (section 5.2).}
\li{\strong{Observation 3}: the excess is not a constant per unit. A third of the units take fewer ticks than in gruszka, 45 \% are within a quarter, and 9 \% of the units (ratio 2 and more) carry half of the excess. By kind: auxiliary proofs and equation lemmas are 1.26 to 1.85 times, the four eager proofs are 10 \% of Mathlib's excess and 28 \% of cslib's, and declarations that repeat an earlier declaration word for word (8 to 11 \% of all units) are 7 to 12 \% of it: gruszka's check facts answer them in about 20 ticks (section 3, 5.3).}
\li{\strong{Proposals}, by measured size (section 6): (1) a closed-pair comparison table that survives sessions; (2) the probe budget of \code{eager} at 2^16; (3) a declaration that repeats an earlier one is not checked again; (4) the cap of \code{congruence} scaled with the unit. Not supported: check facts for terms with level parameters, a different treatment of large proof terms, any rule of gruszka for eager reduction.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\ul{
\li{\strong{RLVM.} A private copy of the tracked sources of the main tree of 2026-11-25 10:50 UTC (the merged tree of rep-INT8), built with \src{scripts/build.sh} as a development distribution without the executable, with measurement aids added to \code{rlvm-machine} only: one line per unit with the differences of the machine's counters over the unit (ticks, records, run words, pair entries, call events and memo hits by op, outcomes of speculative segments), counters at the six tick sites that have no call event, a census by kind of the records the unit made, a census of the callers of four ops, and a shadow table for check facts (section 4.4). The aids are read only under statistics; with them off the build is the main tree's. Run line: the JVM launcher with \code{\verb~|--threads 1 --stats -X stats-file=F -X unit-trace~} and one further setting per experiment. One worker loads in batch and checks in file order. Engine: the machine with its default switch set, policy practical; "session of one declaration" is \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,-warm~}.}
\li{\strong{gruszka.} The arena revision \code{ebc80dc} as built for the baseline tables, and a second build of the same source in the arena container with its own features \code{cnt} and \code{knobs} (counters and switches read from the environment; not profile-guided), with additions in a private copy: ticks by site, values made by kind, the hits and entries of the closed-pair table, a second line per declaration with every counter of its statistics, and five switches (session size in KiB; no check facts; no closed-pair table; no open facts; no shortcut for closed Nat operations in unfolding). Run line: \code{\verb~|SK_DECL_INSTR=1 gruszka FILE.susz 1~} with \code{\verb~|SK_ORDER=file~}, so both checkers go through the file in the same order with one worker; \code{\verb~|SK_SESSION_DECLS=1~} is the session of one declaration.}
\li{\strong{Corpora.} Mathlib (721,845 units, 714,712 checked by the machine), navier-stokes-euler (126,468 and 124,850), cslib (401,985 and 397,608), the exports of the benchmark tables; con-leche, Init, Std, cedar and reachability for two settings.}
\li{\strong{The join.} The unit number of the driver is the index of the declaration record in file order, which is gruszka's index. A unit is joined when the machine accepted it in one run; the 60, 16 and 7 units the reference engine decides and the inductive blocks of the environment thread are left out (gruszka spends 9.4 M, 4.7 M and 2.6 M ticks on the latter).}
\li{\strong{What the numbers are.} Counts of one deterministic run per row. With one worker and file order the machine's totals are 574.1 M ticks on Mathlib, 144.2 M on navier-stokes-euler and 216.0 M on cslib (rep-P11 with 8 workers: 575.8, 145.3 and 216.7 M); gruszka's are 495.5 M, 130.6 M and 188.0 M in file order (rep-P11 with 8 workers in its namespace order: 471.2, 134.5 and 177.8 M; one worker in namespace order here: 131.8 M and 173.8 M on the two small corpora). So the order moves gruszka's total by up to 8 \% and the join compares like with like only in file order.}
\li{\strong{Not a timing.} No run of the instrumented builds is timed. Section 6 has the only new times: the shipped executable and the JVM launcher of the shared distribution of 09:13 UTC against themselves with one setting changed, interleaved inside \src{bench/quiet.py} windows on the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host. Other times are quoted from rep-P11 with their conditions.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The two tick counters}

\p{Both checkers count "ticks". The machine's rule is one tick per judgment that its inline answers (identity, flag, memo, cache) did not answer, plus one per iteration of its loops; a memo hit is never a tick. gruszka ticks at nine sites of its source, and at two of them before the cache is asked. One worker, the whole corpus:}

