\tr{\td{inference past its memo (gruszka also counts leaves)}\td{55.43 M / 81.33 M = 0.68}\td{14.64 M / 18.08 M = 0.81}\td{22.78 M / 35.19 M = 0.65}}
\tr{\td{type of a value past its memo: VTYPE / infer_val_miss}\td{18.08 M / 6.39 M = 2.83}\td{2.90 M / 1.08 M = 2.68}\td{7.55 M / 2.74 M = 2.76}}
\tr{\td{session resets}\td{13,814 / 2,625}\td{3,148 / 561}\td{5,548 / 1,041}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A quarter to a third of gruszka's ticks are inference-cache hits}, which cost one probe, and none of the machine's are. A fifth of the machine's ticks are nodes of inert nests, which gruszka builds without a tick (its canonical first-order applications). "CPU per tick" of the two checkers (rep-P11, section 5.2) compares two different mixtures, and so does the ratio of the totals.}
\li{\strong{Unfoldings.} A \code{DELTA} call is counted after the memo word answered or did not (the hit returns before the tick), so the machine's unfoldings are its \code{DELTA} calls: 24.57 M, 6.22 M and 9.29 M. rep-P11 subtracted the hits from the calls and found 17.9 M, 3.8 M and 7.3 M, "within 3 to 9 \%" of gruszka; against all of gruszka's unfoldings that is 1.23, 1.78 and 1.34 times. gruszka's count includes the unfoldings that go straight to the branch of a casesOn or a matcher (7.1 \%, 4.3 \% and 7.9 \% of them); the machine does those in a case tree, which is a code block and not a \code{DELTA} call. Like for like, unfoldings through a body, the machine is at 1.32, 1.86 and 1.45 times. Every unfolding count of gruszka below is given through a body where it is set against the machine's.}
\li{\strong{Records.} The machine makes 1.8 to 2.2 times as many hash-consed records as gruszka makes values. 27 to 32 \% of its records are suspensions, which are at once the delayed argument and the memo key of evaluation and inference; gruszka keeps that key in two hash maps outside its values (one entry per inference miss). Counted with those entries gruszka makes 239.8 M, 49.7 M and 95.5 M table entries against the machine's 288.3 M, 69.5 M and 112.8 M records: 1.20, 1.40 and 1.18 times. Closures are equal. Neutral applications are 1.43 to 1.68 times gruszka's and fall to 1.12 times on navier-stokes-euler when the machine's session cap is 8 MiB: they are values made again after a reset.}
\li{\strong{whnf and the type of a value} are asked 1.8 to 2.8 times as often past their memo. Three quarters of the machine's whnf calls come from the projection rule of the reduction without unfolding, which reduces the structure by a full whnf (caller census, navier-stokes-euler, session of one declaration: 73.2 \% of 5.96 M calls; 17.8 \% from the one-sided step of lazy delta). Both follow the comparisons that are done again after a reset (section 4): with the 8 MiB cap the machine's whnf calls fall from 2.99 M to 1.86 M on navier-stokes-euler.}
}
}

