\ul{
\li{\strong{No class is at a constant factor.} 34 \% of the units of Mathlib take fewer ticks in the machine than in gruszka (41 \% on cslib, 35 \% on navier-stokes-euler); 45 \% are between 0.8 and 1.25 and hold 57 \% of the machine's ticks at 1.05 times. The units at 2 times and more are 8.9 \% of Mathlib's units, 7.4 \% of cslib's and 10.8 \% of navier-stokes-euler's and carry 50 \%, 77 \% and 72 \% of the excess; there the machine unfolds 4 to 11 times as often as gruszka.}
\li{\strong{By kind}, the excess sits in proofs that reduce: auxiliary proofs (1.26 times on Mathlib, 1.31 on cslib, whnf 1.8 to 2.5 times) and equation lemmas (1.5 to 1.85 times). Structure and inductive auxiliaries, matchers and (on cslib) plain definitions take fewer ticks than in gruszka.}
\li{\strong{By size} the ratio is 1.10 to 1.25 between 100 and 10,000 nodes on all three corpora, 1.13 to 1.18 above that on Mathlib and cslib and 0.71 on navier-stokes-euler, and lowest below 100 nodes on cslib and Mathlib (1.03 and 1.12): there is no per-unit constant. The smallest units show it directly: the 4 axioms of Mathlib take 40 ticks in the machine and 47 in gruszka.}
}
}

