\ul{
\li{\strong{Nothing kept, the two unfold and compare alike.} navier-stokes-euler with a session of one declaration and no cross-declaration fact: 14.25 M unfoldings in the machine, 14.17 M through a body in gruszka (14.72 M in all); 18.25 M and 17.12 M comparisons; 7.65 M and 6.37 M whnf. cslib: 12.07 M unfoldings in the machine and 12.61 M through a body in gruszka (14.56 M in all), 14.56 M and 16.38 M comparisons, 8.20 M and 5.60 M whnf. One declaration, \code{Euler.EulerExistenceAndSmoothness.pressure_smooth}, checked alone by gruszka through its subset mode (every earlier declaration admitted unchecked): 1,432 ticks, 125 unfoldings, 148 comparisons; by the machine with a session of one declaration and no \code{pchk}: 1,410 ticks, 123 unfoldings, 109 comparisons. In gruszka's run of the whole corpus with sessions of one declaration and no check facts the same declaration takes 391 ticks and no unfolding: the closed-pair table answers.}
\li{\strong{The ladder on navier-stokes-euler.} gruszka goes from 14.72 M unfoldings with nothing kept to 5.61 M with the closed-pair table and the open facts, to 5.02 M with the check facts, to 3.50 M with its session. The machine goes from 14.25 M to 11.40 M with \code{pchk}, to 6.22 M with its session of 1 MiB, to 4.24 M at 8 MiB.}
\li{\strong{At the machine's session size and without its closed-pair table gruszka unfolds as often as the machine}: at 1 MiB, through a body, 6.45 M against the machine's 6.22 M on navier-stokes-euler and 9.68 M against 9.29 M on cslib (6.75 M and 11.16 M in all). At its own 8 MiB without the table: 22.22 M through a body against the machine's 24.57 M on Mathlib, 8.26 M against 9.29 M on cslib, 4.18 M against 6.22 M on navier-stokes-euler; the rest is the size of the session.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 gruszka's closed-pair table}

\p{What it is, read from gruszka's source (the entry of its comparison, after the session's pair cache and the structural walk): when both values have no free variable and the pair of their head constants has been seen in the table before, each value gets a \em{name}, the hash-consed id of the substituted, unreduced term it stands for, in a table of the worker that outlives sessions. A pair of closed names that is in the table with a stamp of an earlier declaration is answered from it, "true" or "false". After a comparison that was computed and that unfolded at least once, the pair is written with the number of the declaration, a "false" only if no literal met a constructor that was not declared yet. The table is the worker's own; nothing is shared between workers.}

\table{
\tr{\th{gruszka, one worker, file order}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{entries written, sessions of 8 MiB}\td{304,741}\td{143,615}\td{173,813}}
\tr{\td{hits, sessions of 8 MiB}\td{655,751}\td{208,809}\td{234,705}}
\tr{\td{without the table, sessions of 8 MiB: ticks / unfoldings / whnf / values}\td{+5.8 \% / +22.6 \% / +19.2 \% / +8.0 \%}\td{+5.6 \% / +26.1 \% / +27.4 \% / +8.1 \%}\td{+7.8 \% / +34.8 \% / +25.4 \% / +10.4 \%}}
\tr{\td{entries written / hits, sessions of 1 MiB}\td{not run}\td{149,299 / 413,600}\td{174,998 / 325,478}}
\tr{\td{without the table, sessions of 1 MiB: ticks / unfoldings / whnf / values}\td{not run}\td{+14.2 \% / +63.3 \% / +68.4 \% / +22.3 \%}\td{+11.6 \% / +50.4 \% / +37.0 \% / +15.0 \%}}
}

\p{The pairs are the instance paths of the algebraic hierarchy: \code{\verb~|NormedField.toNormedCommRing Real _ =?= Real.normedCommRing~}, \code{\verb~|Field.toCommRing Real Real.instField =?= Real.commRing~} and the like are the comparisons of the declaration above that gruszka answers from the table. The machine has nothing with this lifetime: its pair table and its memo words end with the session, after 51 units on average on Mathlib (13,814 resets for 714,712 units), 40 on navier-stokes-euler and 72 on cslib, and gruszka's session ends after 275, 225 and 386 units with one worker.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 Check facts and repeated declarations}

\p{gruszka keeps, per shared closed term, that it passed check mode in an earlier declaration under a list of level-parameter names, and then infers it without checking. The machine's \code{pchk} is the same fact for terms without a level parameter. Hits per pass with one worker: gruszka 11.99 M on Mathlib, 1.95 M on navier-stokes-euler, 5.59 M on cslib; the machine 1.22 M, 0.67 M and 0.58 M. In ticks the fact is worth 1.9 \%, 6.2 \% and 2.0 \% to gruszka and 9.2 \% and 1.6 \% to the machine on the two smaller corpora, where it was switched off.}

\p{\strong{The shadow counter.} The instrumented machine kept a table of the closed export terms \em{with} level parameters that passed check mode at the declaration's own levels, and counted every later check-mode inference of such a term in a later declaration, with the ticks of the outermost one:}

\table{
\tr{\th{the machine, default set, one worker}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{closed shared terms with level parameters that were checked}\td{36,036}\td{2,161}\td{15,695}}
\tr{\td{checked again in a later declaration with the same list of level parameters}\td{73,445}\td{1,528}\td{26,770}}
\tr{\td{ticks of the outermost of these inferences (what inferring without checking could save at most)}\td{1.25 M (0.22 \%)}\td{29,151 (0.02 \%)}\td{348,262 (0.16 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the same with any list}\td{1.68 M}\td{44,974}\td{426,295}}
}

\p{So the extension of \code{pchk} to terms with level parameters is worth at most a fifth of a percent of the machine's ticks: inside a session the machine's check stamps already answer these terms, and across sessions they are few.}

\p{\strong{Repeated declarations.} Where gruszka's fact does pay is the root of a declaration. The export holds many declarations twice: the elaborator's auxiliary proofs, matchers and compiler auxiliaries are emitted again under another private name. Counted by identical kind, list of level-parameter names, safety, type id and value id against an \em{earlier} unit:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{units that repeat an earlier unit}\td{59,923 (8.4 \%)}\td{13,336 (10.7 \%)}\td{30,466 (7.7 \%)}}
\tr{\td{their ticks in the machine}\td{7.49 M (1.3 \%)}\td{1.97 M (1.4 \%)}\td{4.13 M (1.9 \%)}}
\tr{\td{their ticks in gruszka}\td{1.06 M}\td{0.18 M}\td{0.54 M}}
\tr{\td{their time inside units in the unit traces of rep-P11 (8 workers, smallest of the later passes): JVM launcher / executable}\td{0.84 s of 74.9 s / 1.07 s (1.1 \%)}\td{0.16 s of 17.7 s / 0.21 s (0.9 \%)}\td{0.40 s of 24.7 s / 0.51 s (1.7 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the largest in the machine}\td{44,499 ticks (gruszka: 20)}\td{15,920 (14)}\td{22,653 (5)}}
\tr{\td{distance to the first occurrence, median, in units}\td{233,976}\td{25,619}\td{133,065}}
}

\p{By kind on Mathlib: 37,282 compiler auxiliaries (2.27 M ticks), 7,475 auxiliary proofs (3.27 M), 6,030 matchers and their lemmas, 6,977 definitions. The repeat is far from its original, so no session holds it.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The three observations}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 The four eager-reduction proofs of \code{Batteries.Data.Char.Basic}}

\p{\code{Char.of_any_eq_false_aux._proof_1_15} and \code{_proof_1_18}, \code{Char.any._proof_1} and \code{Char.all._proof_1} are proofs about \code{\verb~|c + 57343 + 1 < 1114112~} with a variable \code{c}, built by automation; they enter eager reduction, with 1, 3 and 12 deferred comparisons in the three that were looked at.}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{\code{of_any_eq_false_aux._proof_1_15}}\th{\code{_proof_1_18}}\th{\code{Char.any._proof_1}}\th{\code{Char.all._proof_1}}}
\tr{\td{the machine, default set: ticks}\td{3,212,971}\td{3,218,191}\td{982,310}\td{980,211}}
\tr{\td{- of them whnf / reduction without unfolding / code blocks / unfoldings}\td{458,829 / 458,873 / 631,048 / 172,167}\td{459,110 / 459,294 / 631,858 / 172,601}\td{173,080 / 172,818 / 115,875 / 284}\td{172,633 / 172,593 / 115,420 / 259}}
\tr{\td{RLVM's reference engine: steps (the fourth by difference, from the run with the smaller budget)}\td{1,904}\td{8,138}\td{2,923}\td{2,795}}
\tr{\td{gruszka: ticks / unfoldings}\td{1,519 / 177}\td{2,434 / 121}\td{1,467 / 92}\td{2,550 / 228}}
\tr{\td{time in rep-P11 (cslib, 8 workers): executable / JVM launcher / gruszka, ms}\td{531.5 / 282.4 / 0.2}\td{513.6 / 248.7 / 0.2}\td{131.0 / 62.0 / 0.2}\td{150.5 / 87.2 / 0.3}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{It is the machine's probe.} In the plain phase of a declaration that defers eager comparisons the machine runs, at each of the two points where the eager mode would lift a guard, what the eager branch would have done, to record whether the two modes agree ([eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction)). On an open pair that is \code{reduce_nat} or a full whnf of a term with a free variable: here a recursion of 57,344 steps, the literal of the statement, with 3 to 8 whnf calls per step. The statistics say so: the largest probe that completes on cslib and on Mathlib is 2,752,589 ticks, and the next largest on any corpus is 63,654. The official plain procedure never starts that evaluation; RLVM's reference engine, which follows it, needs 1,904 to 8,138 steps.}
\li{\strong{gruszka has no rule for it and no eager mode.} Neither its arena revision nor its present head mentions \code{eagerReduce}; the constant is an ordinary definition to it, and it has no "side is \code{Bool.true}: reduce the other fully" step either. It decides the four by its ordinary lazy delta in the ticks of the table. Switching off its shortcut for closed Nat operations changes none of the four counts.}
\li{\strong{The setting.} \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-probe=N~} is the budget of one probe, 2^22 by default. A probe that is cut leaves its point undecided, the declaration is declined when it enters eager reduction, and the reference engine decides it. One worker, the whole corpus:}
}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{budget 2^22 (default): ticks}\th{2^16: ticks, probes cut, units more for the reference engine, its steps more}\th{2^12}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{574.10 M}\td{565.99 M (-1.41 \%), 4, 4, +15,760}\td{565.47 M (-1.50 \%), 10, 10, +69,133}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{215.96 M}\td{207.84 M (-3.76 \%), 4, 4, +15,760}\td{207.39 M (-3.97 \%), 8, 8, +57,411}}
\tr{\td{con-leche (its one cut probe is cut earlier)}\td{70.76 M}\td{66.63 M (-5.84 \%), 1 as before, 0, 0}\td{66.56 M (-5.92 \%), 1, 0, 0}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{144.22 M}\td{the same, 0}\td{the same, 0}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{34.49 M}\td{the same, 0}\td{34.28 M (-0.60 \%), 4, 4, +41,651}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{56.96 M}\td{the same, 0}\td{56.75 M (-0.37 \%), 4, 4, +41,651}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{76.67 M}\td{the same, 0}\td{76.46 M (-0.27 \%), 4, 4, +41,651}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{9.01 M}\td{the same, 0}\td{the same, 0}}
}

\p{At 2^16 the four proofs cost the machine about 0.28 M ticks before the cut and the reference engine 15,760 steps; the largest probe that still completes (63,654 ticks, four units of Init that every corpus built on it contains) is 3 \% under the budget, so 2^17 is the value with a margin. Which of the two probe sites carries the 2.75 M ticks was not separated.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 The four \code{quadraticBilin} proofs of navier-stokes-euler}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{\code{FiveProfileMoments ._proof_6} (647,064 nodes)}\th{\code{._proof_9} (441,964)}\th{\code{TerminalCompensation ._proof_6} (398,013)}\th{\code{._proof_9} (272,462)}}
\tr{\td{the machine, default: ticks}\td{3,361,005}\td{2,072,461}\td{1,690,798}\td{1,000,660}}
\tr{\td{- unfoldings / lazy delta steps / comparisons / records}\td{164,735 / 262,503 / 207,254 / 948,010}\td{105,335 / 166,139 / 139,628 / 694,119}\td{57,412 / 107,986 / 122,540 / 441,709}\td{31,546 / 58,585 / 81,890 / 324,830}}
\tr{\td{the machine without the cap of \code{congruence} (\code{\verb~|-X machine.cong-spend=0~}; 2^22 gives the same): ticks}\td{2,045,507}\td{1,240,721}\td{1,185,717}\td{725,089}}
\tr{\td{- unfoldings / lazy delta steps / comparisons / records}\td{9,294 / 12,527 / 211,345 / 594,597}\td{6,904 / 8,814 / 142,240 / 470,164}\td{1,506 / 1,949 / 121,660 / 320,408}\td{967 / 1,191 / 81,434 / 258,002}}
\tr{\td{gruszka: ticks}\td{3,068,923}\td{2,059,331}\td{1,782,033}\td{1,192,986}}
\tr{\td{- of them entries of inference / answered by its cache}\td{2,299,664 / 1,921,669}\td{1,542,222 / 1,287,818}\td{1,382,917 / 1,157,651}\td{928,045 / 776,155}}
\tr{\td{- unfoldings / lazy delta iterations / comparisons past the caches / values}\td{8,601 / 47,904 / 116,557 / 276,703}\td{5,839 / 32,103 / 78,065 / 185,741}\td{1,195 / 17,181 / 70,772 / 158,588}\td{916 / 10,693 / 46,529 / 106,695}}
\tr{\td{inference misses: the machine / gruszka}\td{418,134 / 377,823}\td{243,037 / 254,211}\td{232,767 / 225,260}\td{138,721 / 151,825}}
\tr{\td{time in rep-P11 (8 workers; gruszka one worker): executable / JVM launcher / gruszka, ms}\td{744.1 / 521.1 / 236.0}\td{382.5 / 299.2 / 130.9}\td{377.0 / 264.7 / 135.7}\td{188.3 / 146.6 / 69.3}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What gruszka does on a large proof term is what the machine does}: every value hash-consed (2.51 M intern calls for 276,703 values in the largest), the proof's nodes decoded into its arena as exported, inference in check mode of every node once per environment the node reads, the cache answering the shared ones (1.92 M hits). It does not infer only, and it skips no subproof that the machine checks: the inference misses agree within 10 \%.}
\li{\strong{What it does not do is unfold.} The comparisons of these proofs are two applications of one head with large arguments. gruszka compares the arguments first (its structural walk and its argument probes). The machine does the same in a speculative segment (the same-head probe of \code{congruence}), and the segments of one declaration may spend 2^18 ticks in all ([the cap](log-9636)). A proof of 3.4 M ticks uses that up early; from then on the complete procedure runs in the official order and unfolds both sides: 164,735 unfoldings where gruszka needs 8,601. Without the cap the same-head probe answers 117,796 times instead of 12,471, all "true", and the unit needs 9,294 unfoldings.}
\li{\strong{The whole effect of the cap on this corpus is these four units}: 144.22 M ticks with it, 141.29 M without, and the four go from 8.12 M to 5.20 M. On cslib the cap changes nothing (215.96 M both ways), on Mathlib 0.06 \% (574.10 M and 573.74 M). con-leche, for whose heaviest unit it was made, takes 70.76 M ticks with the cap, 72.65 M with 2^22 and 74.73 M without.}
\li{\strong{Why gruszka's tick gets cheaper with size and the machine's dearer.} In the largest proof 75 \% of gruszka's ticks are entries of inference and 63 \% of all its ticks are answered by the inference cache (37 \% over the corpus): its average tick there is a probe. The machine does not tick on those hits, and with the cap in force its ticks there are unfoldings and the comparisons under them. The rest is the size of the session, which is the third lever of rep-P11 and is not touched by anything here: the unit ends with a session of 64 MB with the cap and 43 MB without.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.3 A constant overhead per unit, or classes of units}

\p{Classes. The tables of section 3 show no constant: a third of the units are cheaper in the machine's ticks than in gruszka's, units below 100 nodes are at 1.03 to 1.28 times, and half to three quarters of the excess is carried by the 7 to 11 \% of units at 2 times and more. Of the candidates of the question: \strong{session start}: the machine's one judgment per unit, \code{SIGD}, is 0.1 \% of the ticks, and a reset has no tick; \strong{type inference of binder domains} is not more work than in gruszka (inference misses 0.65 to 0.81 times, gruszka counting leaves); \strong{level instantiation} has no tick in either checker and was not counted; \strong{re-derivation after a session reset} is the cause, and it is not constant per unit: it falls on the units whose comparisons meet closed instance paths and closed computations that an earlier declaration already compared. In numbers of Mathlib's excess of 87.8 M ticks: the four eager proofs 9.5 \%, repeated declarations 7.3 \%, the remaining units at 2 times and more about 33 \%, the units between 1.25 and 2 times 54 \%, those between 0.8 and 1.25 times 16 \%, and the units below 0.8 times give 21 \% back.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Proposals}

\p{Ranked by the size of what each attacks. "Cost only" means the same judgments, cheaper; "strategy" a different order of rules with the official verdict kept, to be gated against the official checker on the hostile suite; "shortcut" something the official kernel does not have, named by the class of [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence) it would fall under. None was implemented. The pass is bound by its total CPU on 8 cores (rep-P11, section 3.1), so a CPU second of the workers is an eighth of a second of the pass.}

\ol{
\li{\p{\strong{A table of comparison results for closed pairs that survives sessions} (what gruszka's closed-pair table is). \em{What gruszka does}: section 4.2. \em{What RLVM does}: the pair table and every memo word end with the session of 1 MiB; comparisons of closed instance paths and closed computations are done again in the next session. \em{Measured size}: without the table gruszka unfolds 23 \% more on Mathlib, 26 \% more on navier-stokes-euler and 35 \% more on cslib at sessions of 8 MiB; at the machine's session size of 1 MiB the table removes 39 \% and 34 \% of gruszka's unfoldings on the two smaller corpora, with 12 \% and 10 \% of its ticks and 18 \% and 13 \% of its values; the machine's unfoldings are within 4 \% of those of gruszka at 1 MiB without the table. \em{Estimated gain}: unfolding and what it triggers is on the stack of 13.9 \% of the workers' CPU on Mathlib, 11.4 \% on navier-stokes-euler and 14.7 \% on cslib (rep-P11, section 2.3, JVM launcher). A third of the unfoldings gone is 4 to 5 \% of the workers' CPU, and gruszka's tick figure says 10 to 12 \%; so 4 to 10 \% of the workers' CPU, 0.4 to 1.0 s of the 13.1 s Mathlib pass. The estimate rests on another checker's counts. \em{Soundness class}: a shortcut, class 3 of the divergence classes (a memo that survives a declaration) with class 2 (a pair table keyed by values), both tolerated kinds and both already taken by warm sessions; a kept "false" can turn into a wrong reject of the machine, which the pipeline's re-check by the reference engine repairs, as it does for the session's own. It must not be written once a declaration has compared under eager reduction, and not after a budget cut. \em{Prototype}: a name for a closed value that does not depend on the session (constant, level list, names of the arguments; a closed export term is a leaf), interned in a table per worker or shared and read-only after publication; the lookup guarded as in gruszka by the pair of head constants, so that an open pair costs one flag test; the gate is the full gate of the machine, the hostile suite with official verdicts under the adversarial pass of [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N), and the differential engine. Whether one shared table beats eight private ones was not measured: gruszka's is private.}}
\li{\p{\strong{The probe budget of \code{eager} at 2^16 or 2^17 ticks instead of 2^22.} \em{Measured size}: 8.11 M ticks less on Mathlib (1.4 \%) and on cslib (3.8 \%), 4.13 M less on con-leche (5.8 \%), nothing on the other five corpora; four units more go to the reference engine on Mathlib and cslib, 15,760 steps. In rep-P11 the four units are 1.33 s of the executable's time inside units on cslib and 1.3 s on Mathlib. \em{Timed}: the table below. \em{Soundness class}: cost only in the verdict. A cut probe is the path the switch already has: the declaration is declined and the reference engine, the engine that follows the official order, decides it. \em{What it takes}: one constant, \code{DEFAULT_EAGER_PROBE} of \code{MachineEngine}; the guard on eight corpora; the eager rows of the hostile suite, which already run with a budget of one tick.}}
\li{\p{\strong{A declaration that repeats an earlier one is not checked again.} \em{What gruszka does}: its check fact for the closed value and type answers the root; 5 to 50 ticks. \em{What RLVM does}: checks it in full, 7.49 M ticks on Mathlib. \em{Measured size}: 8.4 \% of the units of Mathlib, 10.7 \% of navier-stokes-euler, 7.7 \% of cslib; 1.3 to 1.9 \% of the ticks; 0.9 to 1.7 \% of the time inside units (0.84 s of CPU per Mathlib pass on the JVM launcher, 1.07 s in the executable, 0.1 s of the pass). \em{Soundness class}: cost only, in the strongest sense: the official check of a declaration is a function of its kind, level parameters, type and value and of the constants they mention, and its caches live for one declaration, so the official checker computes the second verdict exactly as the first. No waiting is needed: if the first is rejected the file is rejected at or before it. \em{What it takes}: at load, a table from the five-tuple to the first unit (the loader or the driver); the repeat is reported with the verdict of its original. The tick-identity gate does not apply by construction; the verdict gates do.}}
\li{\p{\strong{The cap of \code{congruence} scaled with the unit.} \em{Measured size}: without the cap navier-stokes-euler needs 2.93 M ticks less (2.0 \%), all of it in four units that are 1.69 s of the executable's 23.0 s inside units (rep-P11) and fall from 8.12 M to 5.20 M ticks; Mathlib 0.06 \% less, cslib nothing; con-leche 2.7 \% more with a cap of 2^22 and 5.6 \% more with none. A cap that grows with the nodes of the unit, or with the ticks the unit has used, would keep con-leche's protection and free the giant proofs. \em{Estimated gain}: 0.4 to 0.6 s of CPU on navier-stokes-euler, 2 \% of the pass. \em{Soundness class}: strategy inside a switch that is already in the default set: the segments answer more comparisons from the arguments, the tolerated kind of [the proof-comparison shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts); the hostile suite decides. \em{What it takes}: \code{Conv.congruence} and the setting \code{machine.cong-spend}; what the segments of a giant unit spend without a cap was not counted and is the number the scaling needs.}}
}

\p{\strong{Timed: two settings of the shipped build against itself.} The executable and the JVM launcher of the shared distribution of 2026-11-25 09:13 UTC, a run without options but \code{\verb~|--threads 8 --stats -X passes=N~} and the one setting; warm passes that reload the file, pipelined load; variants interleaved round by round inside one quiet-core window per corpus, three rounds; a cell is the mean over the rounds of the median later pass, in seconds, with the process CPU of that pass in brackets.}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus, launcher}\th{default}\th{\code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-probe=65536~}}\th{\code{\verb~|-X machine.cong-spend=4194304~}}\th{\code{\verb~|-X machine.cong-spend=0~}}\th{rounds}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib, the executable}\td{13.748 (106.5)}\td{13.487 (103.4), -1.9 \%}\td{-}\td{-}\td{3}}
\tr{\td{cslib, the executable}\td{5.324 (40.3)}\td{5.097 (38.7), -4.3 \%}\td{-}\td{-}\td{3}}
\tr{\td{con-leche, the executable}\td{1.447 (9.8)}\td{1.416 (9.1), -2.1 \%}\td{1.482 (10.0), +2.5 \%}\td{-}\td{3}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler, the executable}\td{3.634 (27.6)}\td{-}\td{3.652 (28.1), +0.5 \%}\td{3.588 (27.5), -1.3 \%}\td{3}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib, the JVM launcher}\td{12.208 (93.7)}\td{12.582 (96.1), +3.1 \%}\td{-}\td{-}\td{3}}
\tr{\td{cslib, the JVM launcher}\td{4.561 (34.4)}\td{4.380 (33.3), -4.0 \%}\td{-}\td{-}\td{3}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The probe budget shows where the counts say it should.} cslib: every round with the budget is below every round without it on both launchers, and the process CPU of a pass falls by 1.6 s in the executable, which is what the four units cost there. con-leche gains 2 \% with rounds that overlap. On Mathlib the expected 0.16 s is 1.2 \% of the pass and is not resolved by these windows: in the executable the rounds of one variant differ by up to 1.5 s; on the JVM launcher two of the three rounds with the budget ran with 4 to 8 \% more process CPU than any round without it, for 1.4 \% fewer ticks, and the third is equal (12.21 against 12.13 s). The counts cannot produce that; the load of the host can.}
\li{\strong{The cap of \code{congruence} is not resolved in time}: navier-stokes-euler without the cap is 1.3 \% faster and with a cap of 2^22 0.5 \% slower, inside the scatter of the window, whose first round was 0.4 s slower than the later ones for every variant; con-leche with a cap of 2^22 is 2.5 \% slower, as its ticks say.}
\li{\strong{Peak resident set} of the process, the largest over the rounds, default against the budget of 2^16: cslib 2,008 and 1,600 MiB in the executable, 3,771 and 2,421 MiB on the JVM launcher; con-leche 1,375 and 912 MiB; Mathlib 4,425 and 4,033 MiB, 5,234 and 4,822 MiB. A probe of millions of ticks is one session of that size.}
\li{The windows were taken while other agents measured and built on the other cores of the host; the passes are 4 to 14 \% slower than the same build's rows in rep-INT8. They are a first reading, not the guard of the rule for a default: that needs eight corpora on both launchers.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What the data does not support}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Check facts for closed terms with level parameters}: 0.02 to 0.22 \% of the machine's ticks (section 4.3), although gruszka has 10 times the hits.}
\li{\strong{gruszka's other cross-declaration stores}: the pair cache for closed export terms that are compared as thunks (10,621 hits on navier-stokes-euler) and the open facts for restated bodies (1,265 hits, 31,066 on Mathlib).}
\li{\strong{A rule for eager reduction taken from gruszka}: there is none.}
\li{\strong{A special treatment of large proof terms}: gruszka has none; it decodes, hash-conses and checks them as the machine does.}
\li{\strong{gruszka's shortcut for closed Nat operations in unfolding}: switching it off changes cslib by 1,886 of 188.0 M ticks.}
\li{\strong{A constant to remove per unit}: there is none (section 5.3).}
\li{\strong{The ratio of the tick totals as a measure of work}, in either direction, and "CPU per tick" across the two checkers.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What was not determined}

\ul{
\li{What a closed-pair table gives the machine: it was measured in gruszka only. A shadow count in the machine needs the session-independent name, which is the prototype.}
\li{Why the machine asks whnf 1.8 to 2.3 times as often beyond what the resets explain: at a session cap of 8 MiB it still asks 1.43 times on navier-stokes-euler and 2.0 times on cslib, and with nothing kept on either side 1.20 and 1.46 times. Three quarters of the calls are the projection rule; gruszka's count by caller was not taken.}
\li{Why the machine's neutral records stay 1.34 times gruszka's on cslib at equal session size.}
\li{What the repeated run of a declaration that reaches its first eager argument in a mode-sensitive session costs: 1,502 units of Mathlib, 812 of cslib are run twice; their ticks were not separated.}
\li{Which of the two probe sites of \code{eager} the 2.75 M ticks belong to, and what the segments of \code{congruence} spend in a giant unit without a cap.}
\li{Mathlib with sessions of one declaration and with gruszka's session size varied; gruszka with 8 workers (its closed-pair table and sessions are per worker, so its hits are lower there); anything on x86-64.}
\li{What an event costs. In the arena's metric the executable needs 3.1 to 3.6 times gruszka's instructions on these corpora (rep-INT8), far above every ratio of matched events here (1.0 to 2.3), so per event the machine executes more instructions; no instruction count per event or per unit was taken.}
\li{Times: every row but the table of section 6 is a count. The counter build of gruszka is not its measured binary.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Tools}

\p{All outside the repository. For the machine: a per-unit counter line, the tick-site counters, the record census, the caller census and the shadow table, behind system properties of a private build of \code{rlvm-machine}. For gruszka: a private copy of the arena revision built with its features \code{cnt} and \code{knobs}, with ticks by site, values by kind, the counters of its statistics per declaration and five switches. A filter from gruszka's per-declaration lines to a table, a loader of both tables, the join by class, size and ratio, the table of repeated declarations (kind, level-parameter names, safety, type id, value id read from the export), an index of the export by expression id with a printer of one declaration, and the generator of the tables of this report. To repeat after a change: run both instrumented builds with one worker in file order on the corpus, and join.}
}
